  "label": "TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 19",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 19 — Find the smallest positive integer making x²-52x-52 positive.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Factor out the scale shared by two terms",
      "body": "<p>Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn></mrow></math>. The question asks only for the first positive integer making this positive.</p>",
      "recap": "Testing integer boundaries may avoid complicated exact roots.",
      "pitfall": "A positive real root need not itself be an integer.",
      "pause": "What sign does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> have when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>52</mn></mrow></math>?",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "Use known factor signs to bound an expression on an interval."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>For every positive integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>52</mn></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Thus all positive integers through 52 fail.</p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>53</mn></mrow></math>, the expression is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>53</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Hence 53 is the least allowed integer.</p><p><strong>Answer E: 53.</strong></p>",
